WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.320
(invigorating music)

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:02.610
- [Shelby] Across the United States,

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:05.540
a political shift is rocking
the two major political parties

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:07.770
and promising to play a pivotal role

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:09.700
in this year's midterm elections.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:12.450
- [Aaron] The shift is
among Latino voters.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:14.387
We're gonna show you a WSJ analysis

00:00:14.387 --> 00:00:17.251
of where this shift happened in 2020.

00:00:17.251 --> 00:00:19.205
- And zoom in on three key areas

00:00:19.205 --> 00:00:22.850
to watch as the midterms approach in 2022.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:23.900
You ready?

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:24.733
- I think I'm ready.

00:00:24.733 --> 00:00:27.042
- So Aaron, you're a WSJ editor

00:00:27.042 --> 00:00:29.317
focused on polling and political data.

00:00:29.317 --> 00:00:31.034
Tell us big picture first.

00:00:31.034 --> 00:00:34.200
Why are shifts in this
demographic so significant?

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.620
- This shift among Latino voters

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:37.020
is one of the most important things

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:40.260
that's happened recently
in American politics.

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.309
And everyone's watching
to see if it's durable.

00:00:42.309 --> 00:00:45.080
Latino voters are one of the biggest

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:47.824
and fastest growing
parts of the electorate

00:00:47.824 --> 00:00:51.301
and they swung a lot away
from the Democratic party

00:00:51.301 --> 00:00:55.210
and toward the Republican party in 2020.

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:56.320
- [Shelby] To better understand

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:58.030
political shifts across America,

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:00.320
WSJ created this map that brought together

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:03.010
electoral, demographic, and economic data

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:04.480
at the neighborhood level.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.378
By doing this, we found
something striking.

00:01:06.378 --> 00:01:08.067
- [Aaron] In the last
presidential election,

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:12.589
3,730 predominantly Latino census tracks

00:01:12.589 --> 00:01:14.230
shifted to the right,

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:16.960
compared to just 352 that shifted left.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:20.612
- Census tracks are boundaries
set by the US Census Bureau

00:01:20.612 --> 00:01:23.683
that average about 4,000
residents per track.

00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:25.760
So Aaron, what do these shifts tell you?

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:27.310
- Well, they tell us that something

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:28.680
is changing in the electorate.

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:31.106
The shift was pervasive,
but it wasn't uniform.

00:01:31.106 --> 00:01:34.600
It happened to different
extents and different places,

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:37.810
but it happened among all
kinds of Latino voters.

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:40.113
- Okay, so let's zoom into
some of these key communities.

00:01:40.113 --> 00:01:41.425
First, we'll go to Texas

00:01:41.425 --> 00:01:43.950
to these neighborhoods
along the Southern border.

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:45.063
So what are we seeing here?

00:01:45.063 --> 00:01:47.302
- [Aaron] What we're seeing
is a map of the vote.

00:01:47.302 --> 00:01:50.191
Not county by county, but
neighborhood by neighborhood.

00:01:50.191 --> 00:01:53.018
Technically, these are
census tracks and in 2016,

00:01:53.018 --> 00:01:55.026
these shades of blue indicate

00:01:55.026 --> 00:01:56.932
that the vote was pretty firmly Democratic

00:01:56.932 --> 00:01:58.720
in most neighborhoods.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:00.820
That's that rich, dark blue that you see.

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:02.033
- [Shelby] All right, so then in 2020,

00:02:02.033 --> 00:02:04.640
you can see those shades
of blue got lighter.

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:05.473
- [Aaron] That's right.

00:02:05.473 --> 00:02:08.020
That signals that the
Democratic candidate, Joe Biden,

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:09.600
still won those neighborhoods,

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:11.058
but he got a smaller share of the vote

00:02:11.058 --> 00:02:12.970
than Democrats got in 2016.

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:15.239
- [Shelby] All right, so if
you take a look at the shift,

00:02:15.239 --> 00:02:16.966
here's what that looks like.

00:02:16.966 --> 00:02:20.305
Almost all of these
neighborhoods shifted right.

00:02:20.305 --> 00:02:21.416
Correct?

00:02:21.416 --> 00:02:22.810
- [Aaron] That's right.

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:25.630
This whole region is
now dark red on the map,

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:27.299
showing that there was a big shift,

00:02:27.299 --> 00:02:32.034
about 20 points or more in many
places toward Donald Trump.

00:02:32.034 --> 00:02:33.885
- What do we know about the shift?

00:02:33.885 --> 00:02:35.280
What motivated it?

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:36.270
- We're still studying it.

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:37.860
A lot of people are studying it,

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:39.510
but it looks like the most
important thing that happened

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:41.478
is that the issue mix changed.

00:02:41.478 --> 00:02:44.835
In 2016, there was a lot
of talk about immigration,

00:02:44.835 --> 00:02:48.437
but in 2020, it was all
about the economy and COVID.

00:02:48.437 --> 00:02:50.768
And when COVID struck, it
hit the Rio Grande valley

00:02:50.768 --> 00:02:53.799
very hard, but voters
hold the kind of jobs

00:02:53.799 --> 00:02:55.860
that you can't do from home.

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:57.009
And so when businesses were shut down,

00:02:57.009 --> 00:03:00.274
they lost work and they favored
the Republican point of view

00:03:00.274 --> 00:03:03.174
on keeping businesses open during COVID,

00:03:03.174 --> 00:03:04.796
rather than the Democratic impulse

00:03:04.796 --> 00:03:08.105
to shut things down in order
to protect public health.

00:03:08.105 --> 00:03:09.455
- This shift is really significant,

00:03:09.455 --> 00:03:11.606
but this part of Texas is not as populated

00:03:11.606 --> 00:03:13.820
as some of those huge cities,

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:15.475
Austin, Dallas, Houston.

00:03:15.475 --> 00:03:17.880
Why is this area so
politically significant?

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:20.064
- If you want a signal of
how important this area is,

00:03:20.064 --> 00:03:22.653
consider that both the
candidates running for governor

00:03:22.653 --> 00:03:25.160
the current governor, Greg Abbott

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:26.333
and his reelection campaign,

00:03:26.333 --> 00:03:28.939
and his Democratic
challenger, Beto O'Rourke,

00:03:28.939 --> 00:03:30.608
both started their campaigns

00:03:30.608 --> 00:03:32.765
either along the Rio Grande Valley

00:03:32.765 --> 00:03:34.596
or elsewhere along the border with Mexico.

00:03:34.596 --> 00:03:36.540
And Republicans are very excited

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:40.114
about the prospect of
flipping three US house seats

00:03:40.114 --> 00:03:43.527
in the Rio Grande Valley that
are now held by Democrats.

00:03:43.527 --> 00:03:45.000
- [Shelby] So these are the kind of voters

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:46.390
that could be responsible

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:48.264
for the balance of power in the house?

00:03:48.264 --> 00:03:49.298
- [Aaron] Absolutely.

00:03:49.298 --> 00:03:51.056
- Okay, now let's go to another state.

00:03:51.056 --> 00:03:52.350
Where do you wanna zoom in?

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:53.552
- Shelby, let's go to Pennsylvania

00:03:53.552 --> 00:03:55.568
and look at the city of Reading.

00:03:55.568 --> 00:03:56.925
These census tracks lie

00:03:56.925 --> 00:03:59.880
about 50 miles Northwest of Philadelphia.

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:02.875
In the 2016 presidential
election, they were dark blue,

00:04:02.875 --> 00:04:06.119
indicating that between 80%
and 90% of the vote there

00:04:06.119 --> 00:04:07.485
was Democratic.

00:04:07.485 --> 00:04:09.191
- But in 2020, again,

00:04:09.191 --> 00:04:11.798
we see those shades of
blue get a little lighter.

00:04:11.798 --> 00:04:14.559
And if you look at the
tracks in terms of the shift,

00:04:14.559 --> 00:04:16.639
they all go red.

00:04:16.639 --> 00:04:18.627
And you traveled to Reading recently.

00:04:18.627 --> 00:04:20.893
- It's very different than
the Rio Grande Valley.

00:04:20.893 --> 00:04:23.634
That's rural. Reading of course is urban.

00:04:23.634 --> 00:04:24.725
And a lot of people moved there

00:04:24.725 --> 00:04:26.660
from Philadelphia and New York.

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:28.482
- And what issues are resonating

00:04:28.482 --> 00:04:30.200
with these voters in Reading?

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:32.075
- Well, the economy was still number one

00:04:32.075 --> 00:04:35.345
and the economy under Trump
gave the Republican party

00:04:35.345 --> 00:04:37.706
a lot of credibility with these voters.

00:04:37.706 --> 00:04:39.463
Another thing I found was that immigration

00:04:39.463 --> 00:04:41.693
is important as an issue here,

00:04:41.693 --> 00:04:44.250
but I found that voters were disillusioned

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:46.050
with both political parties.

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:47.517
They might want tough border security,

00:04:47.517 --> 00:04:50.150
but for those who are
already here in the country,

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:51.702
they want to see a pathway to citizenship.

00:04:51.702 --> 00:04:53.014
- Are there any Democratic policies

00:04:53.014 --> 00:04:55.179
that are turning these voters off?

00:04:55.179 --> 00:04:58.143
- That's a big debate among
Democratic strategists

00:04:58.143 --> 00:05:00.723
and some Democratic
strategists are very worried

00:05:00.723 --> 00:05:03.528
that the party has become too identified

00:05:03.528 --> 00:05:06.321
with liberal policy positions on crime

00:05:06.321 --> 00:05:09.506
and policing and on racial preferences

00:05:09.506 --> 00:05:11.938
and affirmative action.

00:05:11.938 --> 00:05:14.573
And I did hear from a number of voters

00:05:14.573 --> 00:05:16.712
that they thought that
the Democratic party

00:05:16.712 --> 00:05:19.070
was just not focusing on the issues

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:20.180
that were important to them.

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:23.010
- So what is at stake
in Pennsylvania in 2022?

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:24.929
- Well, Pennsylvania has
both an important race

00:05:24.929 --> 00:05:27.348
for the Senate and a governor's race.

00:05:27.348 --> 00:05:29.900
You have a Republican
legislature in Pennsylvania

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:31.783
that wants to change
the rules around voting.

00:05:31.783 --> 00:05:33.770
Having a Democratic
governor would make sure

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:37.075
that the Democrats have a say
in the rules around voting

00:05:37.075 --> 00:05:39.180
and who leads the state can matter

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:42.277
in the campaigning for the
next presidential election.

00:05:42.277 --> 00:05:44.060
- All right, now let's go somewhere else

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:45.578
where there's a different mix of voters

00:05:45.578 --> 00:05:47.093
from the ones in Pennsylvania.

00:05:47.093 --> 00:05:49.231
- Shelby, let's go to south Florida

00:05:49.231 --> 00:05:51.580
and it really is a
different kind of place.

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:53.000
Look at all these census tracks

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:55.004
where more than 70% of
the residents are Latino.

00:05:55.004 --> 00:05:56.607
We're particularly interested

00:05:56.607 --> 00:05:59.910
in the densely populated
neighborhoods around Miami.

00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:01.190
- [Shelby] Wow, so there are a lot

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:02.640
of these little census tracks.

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:04.989
So at first glance,
it's pretty hard to tell

00:06:04.989 --> 00:06:08.577
how much things changed
between 2016 and 2020.

00:06:08.577 --> 00:06:11.162
- [Aaron] But here's where
things get super clarifying.

00:06:11.162 --> 00:06:14.591
Look at the shift between 2016 and 2020.

00:06:14.591 --> 00:06:16.476
All of these red census tracks

00:06:16.476 --> 00:06:19.327
show that voters there
shifted to the right.

00:06:19.327 --> 00:06:21.528
- So in a state where population centers

00:06:21.528 --> 00:06:23.680
are pivotal, this is a big shift.

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:25.138
- Yeah, it is a big shift.

00:06:25.138 --> 00:06:27.060
And it's one reason, important reason,

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:30.955
that Donald Trump won the
state of Florida in 2020.

00:06:30.955 --> 00:06:34.204
And it's also a reason, the
shift in the Latino vote,

00:06:34.204 --> 00:06:37.044
that Republicans picked up two house seats

00:06:37.044 --> 00:06:40.435
in south Florida that had
been held by Democrats.

00:06:40.435 --> 00:06:42.120
- When you think about Miami,

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:43.387
you often think about Cuban Americans,

00:06:43.387 --> 00:06:46.402
but the Latino vote there
is quite diverse, right?

00:06:46.402 --> 00:06:47.680
- It is.

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:49.983
But the large presence in south Florida

00:06:49.983 --> 00:06:52.875
of Latino voters who come from countries

00:06:52.875 --> 00:06:55.539
that are dictatorships,
Cuba and Venezuela,

00:06:55.539 --> 00:06:59.382
noticeably meant that one
issue had particular salience

00:06:59.382 --> 00:07:00.557
in south Florida,

00:07:00.557 --> 00:07:03.487
and that is the Republican
criticism that Democrats

00:07:03.487 --> 00:07:07.208
are gonna turn the United
States into a socialist country.

00:07:07.208 --> 00:07:09.490
- So what's at stake in the midterms?

00:07:09.490 --> 00:07:11.788
- Well Florida has both a governor's race

00:07:11.788 --> 00:07:13.768
and a Senate race this year.

00:07:13.768 --> 00:07:16.255
And then when you come to house districts

00:07:16.255 --> 00:07:17.778
like the two in south Florida

00:07:17.778 --> 00:07:19.571
that flip to the Republican party,

00:07:19.571 --> 00:07:20.880
this year,

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:23.721
it's unlikely that Democrats
can win those seats back.

00:07:23.721 --> 00:07:26.638
The analysts now rate
them as solidly Republican

00:07:26.638 --> 00:07:29.365
in part because of the
shift in the Latino vote.

00:07:29.365 --> 00:07:30.736
- So zooming back out,

00:07:30.736 --> 00:07:32.370
these maps we're looking at

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:34.291
show a shift between
presidential elections.

00:07:34.291 --> 00:07:36.434
Do analysts think that will matter

00:07:36.434 --> 00:07:38.400
in terms of the Latino vote?

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:40.228
- Well, what we have now is polling

00:07:40.228 --> 00:07:42.040
and we have focus groups

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:45.270
and they suggest that, yes,
what we're seeing in 2020,

00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:47.920
this shift toward the
Republican party is continuing.

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:51.307
- Can we see how issues are
shaping the midterms already?

00:07:51.307 --> 00:07:53.776
- You can see how the two parties

00:07:53.776 --> 00:07:56.776
are trying to shape the issue landscape.

00:07:56.776 --> 00:07:59.050
- We're gonna be the most
prosperous state in the nation.

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:00.244
We're gonna open up our energy sector

00:08:00.244 --> 00:08:01.900
like you'd never seen it before.

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:04.340
- We're gonna work at the
local state and federal level

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:07.911
to continue to support a
woman's right to choose.

00:08:07.911 --> 00:08:09.739
- We are making sure that we are going

00:08:09.739 --> 00:08:12.470
after the criminal element
that's coming across the border.

00:08:12.470 --> 00:08:14.525
- The time for us to stop
the next mass shooting

00:08:14.525 --> 00:08:18.970
in this country is right
now, right here, today.

00:08:18.970 --> 00:08:21.180
- People might have
opinions about abortion,

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:23.000
they might have opinions
about the economy,

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:24.765
they might have opinions
about immigration,

00:08:24.765 --> 00:08:26.205
but that doesn't mean that that issue

00:08:26.205 --> 00:08:28.569
is the one that's gonna
make them come out and vote

00:08:28.569 --> 00:08:31.781
and is gonna determine
their choice of candidates.

00:08:31.781 --> 00:08:35.360
Which issues are most salient
is still an open question.

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:36.410
- Why does this kind of shift

00:08:36.410 --> 00:08:37.818
matter for the political parties?

00:08:37.818 --> 00:08:39.320
- If this shift is durable,

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:42.450
it marks a big change for
the two political parties.

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:43.906
If Latino voters are shifting away

00:08:43.906 --> 00:08:45.349
from the Democratic party

00:08:45.349 --> 00:08:46.981
and becoming more of a swing group,

00:08:46.981 --> 00:08:48.711
that means that one of the pillars

00:08:48.711 --> 00:08:51.300
of the Democratic
coalition is getting shaky.

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:53.620
Republicans on the other hand,

00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:54.916
they've become in large part,

00:08:54.916 --> 00:08:57.079
the party of America's
white working class,

00:08:57.079 --> 00:08:59.625
but they wanna be a
party of a working class

00:08:59.625 --> 00:09:01.762
across all racial groups.

00:09:01.762 --> 00:09:03.566
This movement among Latino voters,

00:09:03.566 --> 00:09:04.721
if it continues,

00:09:04.721 --> 00:09:07.036
suggests that this is in fact happening

00:09:07.036 --> 00:09:09.180
and the Republican party can become

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:10.710
the party of America's future

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:12.653
by building across different races,

00:09:12.653 --> 00:09:14.671
a party of America's working class.

00:09:14.671 --> 00:09:17.754
(invigorating music)

